Ermm ... I disagree. I'm playing a *chaotic* character, not an *evil*
character.

Going from the D&D manual, IIRC ... a *chaotic* character is someone
who makes up his own rules as he goes along and pays no attention to
the rules of the larger outside society.

E.g. "I don't kill peacefuls because I don't want to"

is a valid chaotic response.

"I don't kill peacefuls because it's against the law"

is NOT a valid chaotic response. A chaotic doesn't care two cents for
the law one way or the other.

And thus a chaotic way of life can embrace both good people (who are
committed to, say, loving the other fellow as they do themselves, and
who refrain from murdering peacefuls because it doesn't fit their
character) and evil people (who murder peacefuls because they want to,
or because they got in their way, or ...)


Good is not lawful and chaotic is not evil. That is why Baalzebub is
lawful and  Grey-elves are chaotic.

Maybe I'm wrong .. but I had the distinct impressions that all the
player gods could be "good" ... it's just that the Chaotic player has
more latitude as to what they can and cannot get away with.  The only
truly evil side is Moloch's side, and the player isn't allowed to join
that side.

